1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,356
You fixed one thing.

2
00:00:01,636 --> 00:00:04,619
And while you were in the same file, you changed something else.

3
00:00:04,899 --> 00:00:07,024
Now Git sees one big edit.

4
00:00:07,304 --> 00:00:09,800
If you commit it all together, your history gets muddy.

5
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:11,694
Which change was this commit about?

6
00:00:11,974 --> 00:00:17,047
In this video, you will learn how to stage just one hunk in GitTree, and commit only that part.

7
00:00:18,217 --> 00:00:19,447
First, what is a hunk?

8
00:00:19,727 --> 00:00:24,348
When Git compares two versions of a file, it groups the changed lines into blocks.

9
00:00:24,628 --> 00:00:28,668
Each block, together with a few unchanged lines around it, is a hunk.

10
00:00:28,948 --> 00:00:30,342
In our file there are two.

11
00:00:30,622 --> 00:00:32,851
The first is a new comment above the class.

12
00:00:33,131 --> 00:00:35,337
The second adds a limit to the query function.

13
00:00:36,517 --> 00:00:38,502
Git keeps your work in three places.

14
00:00:38,782 --> 00:00:40,814
The working tree holds the files you edit.

15
00:00:41,094 --> 00:00:43,961
The staging area holds what the next commit will contain.

16
00:00:44,241 --> 00:00:46,145
And the repository holds your commits.

17
00:00:46,425 --> 00:00:49,049
A commit takes only what is in the staging area.

18
00:00:49,329 --> 00:00:54,031
So to commit one hunk, move just that hunk into the staging area, then commit.

19
00:00:54,311 --> 00:00:56,204
The other hunk stays in your working tree.

20
00:00:57,383 --> 00:01:00,414
Here is GitTree, with a small demo project open.

21
00:01:00,694 --> 00:01:04,792
At the top of the graph, the working changes row tells us that files have changed.

22
00:01:05,072 --> 00:01:05,548
Click it.

23
00:01:06,717 --> 00:01:07,831
Select the file.

24
00:01:08,111 --> 00:01:11,432
GitTree shows its diff, already split into hunks.

25
00:01:11,712 --> 00:01:13,035
Hunk one is the comment.

26
00:01:13,315 --> 00:01:15,057
Hunk two is the query change.

27
00:01:16,217 --> 00:01:18,434
Every hunk has its own Stage Hunk button.

28
00:01:18,714 --> 00:01:21,965
Click it on the first hunk, and that hunk moves to the staging area.

29
00:01:23,117 --> 00:01:24,789
Now look at the file lists.

30
00:01:25,069 --> 00:01:26,833
The same file appears twice.

31
00:01:27,113 --> 00:01:30,608
Under Unstaged Files, it still holds the hunk we left behind.

32
00:01:30,888 --> 00:01:33,628
Under Staged Files, it holds the hunk we picked.

33
00:01:34,783 --> 00:01:37,755
Click the staged copy to see exactly what will be committed.

34
00:01:38,035 --> 00:01:39,278
Only the comment is there.

35
00:01:40,450 --> 00:01:42,250
Write a commit message, and commit.

36
00:01:42,530 --> 00:01:44,840
This commit contains only the comment change.

37
00:01:46,017 --> 00:01:49,523
The other hunk is still in your working changes, ready for its own commit.

38
00:01:50,683 --> 00:01:53,505
Close the diff, and select the new commit in the graph.

39
00:01:53,785 --> 00:01:56,490
It changed one file, with a single added line.

40
00:01:56,770 --> 00:01:58,859
That is how you keep every commit about one thing.

41
00:02:00,017 --> 00:02:03,488
In plain Git, the same job is done with git add -p.

42
00:02:03,768 --> 00:02:06,253
Git shows you each hunk, and asks what to do.

43
00:02:06,533 --> 00:02:10,735
Press y to stage it, n to skip it, and s to split it into smaller hunks.

44
00:02:11,015 --> 00:02:13,210
Here we staged the first and skipped the second.

45
00:02:13,490 --> 00:02:16,961
Then git diff --staged shows what the commit will contain.

46
00:02:17,241 --> 00:02:19,795
GitTree does the same, with one click per hunk.

47
00:02:20,950 --> 00:02:21,716
Two tips.

48
00:02:21,996 --> 00:02:26,513
First, if you change your mind, the same button reads Unstage Hunk on the staged side.

49
00:02:26,793 --> 00:02:32,029
Second, if one hunk mixes two ideas, pick individual lines instead of the whole hunk.

50
00:02:32,309 --> 00:02:33,446
That is the next video.

51
00:02:34,617 --> 00:02:35,128
To recap.

52
00:02:35,408 --> 00:02:37,846
A hunk is a block of changed lines.

53
00:02:38,126 --> 00:02:40,471
Stage Hunk moves it to the staging area.

54
00:02:40,751 --> 00:02:42,713
And a commit takes only what is staged.

55
00:02:42,993 --> 00:02:47,219
Download GitTree for free at gittree.app, and see you in the next video.

